import Native

-- What `swap` and `exchange` are refused for. Each function is one rule; the expectation beside
-- this file is the whole of what it reports.

data Handle {value: I64}

-- Both places are written, so both are exclusive for as long as the operation runs. `swap(x, x)` is
-- the smallest program that asks for one storage to be two of them, and it is rejected by the
-- exclusivity rule that was there before swap existed rather than by anything written for it. That
-- is the whole reason these take their places from `&` arguments.
fn selfSwap() -> I64:
    let &value = 1 :: I64
    swap(value, value)
    return value

-- An immutable binding names storage nothing may write, which is as true of an exchange as of an
-- assignment: what these do to a place is write it.
fn immutableSwap() -> I64:
    let x = 1 :: I64
    let &y = 2 :: I64
    swap(x, y)
    return x

fn immutableExchange() -> I64:
    let slot = Handle {value: 1}
    let old = exchange(slot, Handle {value: 2})
    return old.value

{-
   A subscript in `&` position, which does not work and is not about swap.

   `xs[0] = v` routes through `getMut` because resolvePlace has a case for a subscript; an argument
   is resolved as an ordinary expression first and reaches `get`, whose result is an immutable
   borrow. So what arrives at the `&` parameter is a place that may not be written, and the
   diagnostic is the one any immutable argument gets.

   The ordering is what makes this more than an oversight: which accessor a subscript wants is
   decided by the parameter's convention, and the convention is not known until overload resolution
   has picked a callee, which needs the argument types. Every `&` parameter has this - it is not
   reached by swap, only shown by it - and a collection that wants an exchange of its own element
   offers an operation rather than leaving callers to write this.
-}
fn element() -> I64:
    let &xs = [1, 2, 3]
    let old = exchange(xs[0], 9)
    return old

fn twoElements() -> I64:
    let &xs = [1, 2, 3]
    swap(xs[0], xs[2])
    return xs[0]
